Found it! Two issues:
1. The `assert.strictEqual` on DOM elements caused an infinite loop in assert's error message formatting. Fixed by using `assert(ae === editInput, ...)`.
2. The `document.activeElement` is `BODY`, not the edit input. This means the `focus()` call in the app isn't working as expected.

Let me look at why focus isn't working. In my app's `handleListDblClick`:
```js
editingTodoId = id;
render();
var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
if (editInput) {
  editInput.focus();
}
```

But `li` here is the OLD li (from before render replaced innerHTML). After render, the old li is detached from the DOM. `li.querySelector('.edit')` finds the edit input on the OLD detached li. Calling `focus()` on that detached element doesn't set `document.activeElement` because it's not in the DOM.

I need to fix the app code to find the edit input on the NEW li after render:

```js
function handleListDblClick(e) {
  var li = e.target.closest('li');
  if (!li) return;
  if (e.target.tagName.toLowerCase() === 'label') {
    var id = parseInt(li.getAttribute('data-id'), 10);
    editingTodoId = id;
    render();
    // Find the edit input in the NEW DOM (after render replaced innerHTML)
    var todoList = document.querySelector('.todo-list');
    var newLi = todoList.querySelector('li[data-id="' + id + '"]');
    if (newLi) {
      var editInput = newLi.querySelector('.edit');
      if (editInput) {
        editInput.focus();
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Let me fix this in app.js:

